From caf-talk Caf Apr 11 05:56:17 1994
Newsgroups: comp.org.eff.talk,alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk,alt.wired
From: juggle@sage.cc.purdue.edu (PUJC)
Subject: Re: cdrom lifting FAQs from inet w/o permission
Message-ID: <Co3946.tAt@mozo.cc.purdue.edu>
Date: Mon, 11 Apr 1994 09:31:18 GMT

Being copyrighted does not preclude being freely distributable.
Most of the freely distributed programs available on the net
still retain the copyright of the author(s), but remain freely
distributable (usually at their request.)  It seems that FAQs
are posted to their respective newsgroups in order to freely
provide basic information to its readers.  Disseminating
information freely in a public forum is not something to do
with information you do not want repeated or copied freely.
Th nature of an FAQ (IMHO) is to freely provide information
at no charge.  That's why they're commonly available by
anonymous FTP and posted frequently.
   As with Bruce Sterling's "Hacker Crackdown" which is 
currently available from many FTP sources (including
ftp.cdrom.com), the information is freely distributable
so long as the information is not charged for, merely the
medium.
   If you post an FAQ to usenet, and someone has to pay
their on-line service to read that FAQ, should you get
a portion of that?  No, since they aren't charging for
the information, per se, but the access to that information.
--
Dan

