Subject: edupage-04.07.94

THE DROP OF A PIN
A sweepstakes promotion sponsored by Nynex-owned New York Telephone used
used replicas of customers' calling cards, including their secret personal
identification numbers, or PINs. The mailing has caused an uproar among
some of the three million recipients, who worry about unauthorized use of
their calling cards. Nynex has offered to change the PINs of customers who
complain and cover any fraudulent charges. (New York Times 4/7/94 A1)

DIALOG HAS NEW FEE STRUCTURE FOR ON-LINE INFO
Dialog is going to sell its clients the right to share documents with
coworkers, and its fees will be based on the number of people within an
organization who are authorized to read documents downloaded from Dialog's
system. (New York Times 4/6/94 D6)
[...]
BILLBOARDS ON THE HIGHWAY
It had to happen -- sheer numbers and demographics of on-line users make
advertising on electronic networks inevitable. The debate is now focused on
which model is least offensive to customers. While America Online is
experimenting with retrievable ads and an electronic "shopping cart,"
Compuserve offers an electronic mall featuring the wares of 130 catalogs
and direct marketers. The electronic version of the Atlanta Journal and
Constitution is pioneering on-line classifieds and employment ads. "The
whole world of advertsising will get turned upside-down over the next
decade," notes the president of America Online. (Investor's Business Daily
4/7/94 p.4) 

