Well, I have not seen the CD in question until now, so I can't
comment on its additional content. But I can conment on something else:

- Walnut Creek's high-volume CD's (SIMTEL, CICA, Hobbes) go at $25 each, 
  remarkably less if you buy in quantities.  I do not consider this a 
  ripoff but a fair price - actually a good deal, even though I've got
  a quite nice internet connectivity at this site. I don't have numbers
  for their other CDs, but depending on the quantities you make cost 
  can be remarkably more if you make less. Add to that general cost
  of their business, and you get quite understandable prices.
  BTW - Adding s+h into the cd price is not fair at all. You pay
  $5 as well (once!) if you order any other number of CDs. I think that's
  reasonable.

- I seem to remember a Walnut Creek policy that every contributor to
  a CD they publish can get a free copy.  Ask.

- We are talking about large number of data. On their archive CDs they
  have really thousands and thousands of files. Requiring them to obtain
  expressed permission from every single author would invoolve such
  a massive amount of work that it would effectively forbid the publication
  of those CDs - or increase costs beyond every reasonable value. What you 
  require here is a killing requirement - like the owner of an ftp site 
  mirroring some other sites having to ask each file contributor for 
  permission to mirror. 


