References: <2nv5r2$401@chaos.dac.neu.edu> <2o2o2u$126@chaos.dac.neu.edu>

Regards distributing FAQs via CDROM, and those who dislike it:

I pay for my CDROMs, *and* I pay for my feed.  In both cases, money goes
to organizations that move bits for a profit.  Perhaps you don't pay for
your feed, in which case it may be possible that I am paying to move your
bits with my taxes.   I'll make you a deal - if you let me keep on getting
bits via plastic, I'll let you keep on getting bits via wires.  

Think about it ... bits moved on wire cost money to store on hard disk at
both ends of the link, and the bits cost money to move.  If I had infinite
bandwidth, and lots of time, and a much better program than archie to go
looking with, I suppose I could just look for faqs over the net.  As it is,
for those of us with limited bandwidth that we pay for ourselves, it is far
easier to yank a CDROM out of the drawer.  Much better organized, too.
Sometimes out of date.  Oh well.

If the folks turning out the CDROMs are trying to copyright the FAQs
themselves, then beat them with a stick.  However, there is nothing
stopping you from taking all those FAQs and putting them on your own
CDROMs and giving them away to everyone for free, thus putting the
Evil-profit-grabbing-minions-of-capitalism (TM) out of business.
Of course, you go bankrupt too, but that's virtuous, isn't it?  :-)

While you are at it, get that Ansel Adams guy.  Making those expensive
coffee table books full of beautiful photographs, with the images 
*stolen* from public domain forests and parks.  Those images should
only be available for "special" people, who have the time and money
to go to the source.  Ooops.  Your not a "special" person?  Neither am I.

These disks run $15-$40 a piece, and the market is small, so nobody is
getting rich as Bill Gates doing this.  If you dislike people making
money, attack him.  Or Hillary.  Kindly leave my suppliers alone.
Thank you *very* much.

Keith
