In article <frostCo04Fz.2A3@netcom.com>, John Frost <frost@netcom.com> wrote:
>I wish I had time to respond in depth to all the articles that have been
>posted over the last few days. But I don't. So take this summary 
>statement instead.

I'll try and present the opposing viewpoint, at least as far as I see it.
This isn't a flame, this is a complement to the original post.

>1) there is a huge split between people who think information should be
>free to distribute, package, manipulate no matter who makes money off of
>it, how it is distributed, with what ever other information, etc. and the
>people who think that information should be free and therefore want to
>control the amount of money charged for it, where it can be found, the
>accuracy of the information, the style, the associates, the general
>atmosphere and environment around the information that has been culled
>and formed into a coherent document, namely a FAQ. 

I think the crucial word here is *free*.  Information is never free - 
there's always money being exchanged for it.  You pay Netcom $17.50
(or more) for their service, which includes USENET.  Just because
you don't pay per byte of news traffic you read or post doesn't make it
free.  Don't they have that disclaimer that gets made when one posts
to a newsgroup saying "this message will be sent to machines throughout
the entire civilized world and will cost the net hundreds if not thousands
of dollars to send - are you sure you want to do this?"?  

To me, it sounds like by agreeing to the above terms, you have given
permission for your post to be redistributed to machines anywhere and
everywhere.  It does not specify by which mechanism it gets transferred.
You have a very strong case in saying that the above question doesn't
remove your right to restrict its print publication, but in effect, by
posting, you have given up that right.

>This appears to be a
>pretty strong division and will likely result in many strong debates,
>because at first the difference appears to just be a failure of the other
>party to understand where one is coming from. But when it comes down to
>it, this gap is as big as being pro-choice and anti-choice.

It's not an idealogical issue, really, and certainly not a religious
issue, so I don't see the comparison to abortion.  It might be an 
ethical issue, in that *ethically*, I will agree that it would have been
proper for Walnut Creek to at least inform the publishers of FAQs
it is going to publish them.  But on the other hand, rtfm.mit.edu 
doesn't ask (I don't think... though I could be wrong.  They never 
asked to hold my FAQ :)
 
>2) regarding copyright law. Although I have yet to get a final legal
>opinion (which in the end will have to come from the courts, if it goes
>that far), it appears that FAQ authors/maintainers/etc do have a de-facto
>copyright on their material. Legally, if somone wants to use that
>material, they should ask and receive permission first. 

How do you reconcile this with the basic functioning of news systems?
It would be next to impossible for every news server to explicitly 
ask for permission to "use" (which I equate with store for its users
to read) a particular document.  This is why there's that statement when
you post something, which seems to give a blanket permission to give
up control over redistribution to other machines.  I won't pretend to
be a lawyer, but I'd be surprised if the legal community didn't see
this point at least partially.

It'd be interesting if someone could dig up some documents or discussion
from the creaters of NNTP around the time distributted news systems
developed over these issues.  Actually, is there anything like this
in the news RFC's?

(the shaky legal position of copyrights deleted)

>3) Usenet Distribution - Unlike trademarks, where every known violation
>must be prosecuted or risk losing the trademark, copyright is a flexible
>commodity. One can pick and choose their battles or define the copyright
>in any way and not lose the right to control who publishes it in the
>future. So if a copyright notices reads this may be distributed on USENET
>and a limited number of FTP sites, but may not be packaged with other
>materail for commercial sale. Then that notice holds.

True.  However, unless USENET is explicitely defined in your FAQ to be
exactly how you seem to believe it is, i.e. "machines talking to each
other over NNTP", then you might have a case, although you'd never
have a chance to limit its redistribution to UUCP sites, on DAT to
Australia, etc.  Further, if you were to define USENET as it sounds
you think it is, i.e. "machines talking to each other via NNTP with no
financial compensation taking place for the cost of transmission",
then ironically enough it wouldn't even be able to leave Netcom!

So where do we look for a real, well-understood definition of
USENET? I would say that the strongest suggestion of a definition
is the "machines throughout the civilized world" statement you
agree to when posting.  Certainly, if you limited your distribution
to netcom, that might be considered an explicit statement that you
didn't want it reposted outside of netcom.

>4) Walnut Creek CDROM, the offending company, is not a big evil super
>powered publisher, like Prentice House or Sony. It is an Internet aware
>and friendly company, except in this case they goofed. They should have
>asked for and received permission for each FAQ that didn't explicitly say
>"this may be distributed and/or on CDROMS." This goof shouldn't be a
>death penalty for their company. Indeed the have an 800# where if you
>call they will remove the FAQ you created.

Sound to me like they have taken steps to be in line with the
ethical question, which for me is the only question in this debate.

>cd will be changed. And still this doesn't prevent any other company from
>doing the same thing Walnut Creek has done, and chances are they won't be
>so friendly. My Opinion is that we should put a stop to it now.

But you can't.  It's exactly like record companies trying to control
home taping, and they couldn't do it legally so they decided to put a
"copyright tax" on blank DAT tapes (what eedziots).  You cannot really
dispute the following statement: In order for someone to view your
FAQ, they have to somehow pay a third party to access it.  Whether
that third party is netcom, their school (paid for by tuition), their
job (employee benefits), even freenets (paid for by contributions),
somewhere, money gets transferred to someone other than you for your
work.  Information in the electronic age may come very cheap, but it
is never without some cost somewhere.  Unless, of course, you're not
paying your netcom bills :)

	Brian

