Yes it does.

Rtfm.mit.edu was set up by the moderator of news.answers to cache
"pre-approved" FAQs.  My FAQ is archived on rtfm.mit.edu because I
*asked* the moderator to let me distribute it via news.answers and
rtfm.mit.edu (and echoes).  It is all part of a system:  when the
new version of my FAQ goes out with Supersedes in the header, the
old version is replaced with the new one.  File names are the same
everywhere.  Indexing is provided.  Help is provided.  Walnut Creek
provides none of these features.

Walnut Creek did something un-ethical and un-professional.  Because
they are generally well regarded, most FAQ authors who object to
the CD seem content to have their FAQs removed from future copies.
John Frost is concerned with the *precedent*, however, and I think
he has a point (see below).

Defenders of Walnut Creek take note:  Just because you like them
does not make them right:  that's the essence of bigotry.

Re "public domain", I too thought that it simply meant the author
gave up all copyrights, and it had nothing to do with how others
might copy or distribute it.  But I have been assured by a US Gov't
employee that it is otherwise.  As it is outside my experience or
interest, I'll leave this issue to the experts, except to say this:
some readers believe that anything published via Usenet is auto-
matically in the public domain.  I don't think this is so, but if
it is ever found to be true, in a court of law, then that will be
the last day I ever contribute to Usenet.

