>groups are and how much space to set aside for them is going to have problems 
>of the very real, non-ethical sort, for example.

I'll be happy to let my admin know you think he is a doofus.  The system
here has worked very well so far.  He lets the users decide which groups
are worthwhile, instead of either having to spend the time to guess, or
newgrouping everything with the attendant problems that causes.  Saves
disk space, saves time, and preserves freedom of access.  

Up till now, newsgroups were for discussion, and exchanging ideas, and
if there was a lot of overhead from flames and trash, well, it just meant
a little extra disk space.  Usenet is considered valuable enough to 
commit to carrying that overhead in order to promote free inquiry.  
The admins are generally aware of the problems generated by some groups
like alt.binaries, but since bloat and overrunning a disk can happen
in any group, a smart admin puts automatic brakes on to stem the flow
until the problem can be examined more closely by a human.  Up till
now, that has been the only danger from adding a new group, and is
considered worth it to fulfill the academic mission of the university.

But the explict use of a newsgroup to censor posts is something that
hasn't had to be dealt with before.  Now, instead of using up a bit
of disk space, admins are going to have to deal with people telling
them that carrying certain newsgroups is "implied consent" to censor
certain posters.  No more will we be able to use our fine system of
letting users decide which groups should be carried.  One user 
requesting alt.cancel.bots would result in a denial of access to
the Serdar posts for everybody, including someone who might be studying
the bot as part of a sociological inquiry.  Or suppose someone creates
a group to cancel all Dan Gannon posts (remember, you consent to it
if you carry the group, so this is all kosher---merely a way to help
sysadmins control their feeds, right?), a user requests it and poof
there goes the source for some psychology professors work.

How about if someone just starts cancelling articles without making
a new group, instead sending out an article that says, "I'm gonna do
this, if you don't like it, don't accept my cancels, otherwise you
consent to have this done".  Isn't an admin responsible for the posts
they choose to accept on their system as much as they are for the 
groups they accept?  After all, groups are merely a way to partition
posts conveniently.

Not to mention that there is a far better way for an admin to shut the
bot up on their system.  They can have their upstream feed alias their
site to anatolia, and poof, there goes Serdar.  The newsgroup method
is only going to work for sites where the news software doesn't check
the cancel from against the article from anyway.  It is an inferior
method, and comes with a lot of headaches and problems that aliasing
doesn't.


