Apropos this observation, Alexis de Tocqueville (Phillips Bradley revision of
the Henry Reeve text) states:  "I know of no country in which there is so
little independence of mind and real freedom of discussion as in America."
He goes on to indicate that this is because when a majority, or even a vocal
minority, in this country reaches some conclusion on a public issue, that
conclusion is invested with a moral force which does not allow disagreement.
Public officials, with this power at their backs, "dare do things that even
a European, accustomed as he is to arbitrary power, is astonished at.  By this
means habits are formed in the heart of a free country which may some day
prove fatal to its liberties."  [These quotes are from chapter XV, UNLIMITED
POWER OF THE MAJORITY IN THE UNITED STATES, AND ITS CONSEQUENCES.]

Remember, now, that de Tocqueville was writing in the 1840's, and see how
deeply rooted this tendency is in our body politic.

Recall also a statement by Salman Rushdie with regard to his book "The
Satanic Verses," the subject of recent and continuing calumny:  "The right
to free speech includes the right to offend.  Without it, free speech is a
myth."  These remarks appear in a pamphlet he wrote entitled "In Good Faith."

I conclude with another observation from de Tocqueville, from the same source
as above.  Note that his words "majority" and "minority" are used in their
earlier senses; they do not have the racial or gender significance which they
more recently have acquired.  "If ever the free institutions of America are
destroyed, that event may be attributed to the omnipotence of the majority,
which may at some future time urge the minorities to desperation and oblige
them to have recourse to physical force.  Anarchy will then be the result, but
it will have been brought about by despotism."  This has already happened once
in our country, and may yet happen again.  Those who look to the government and
the force of law to insulate them from whatever they find discomfiting, and
these are not few in modern times, do not understand the nature of freedom, and
run a terrible risk.  Evolution is generally preferable to revolution, even if
it is not nearly so exciting.

I find the screed which gave rise to this discussion peculiarly offensive, but
my head is mounted on a swivel -- like everyone else's.  If I don't like what
I am looking at, I can turn it in another direction.

Neil Dickey     E-mail: neil@geol.niu.edu


