I want to respond to the article from LaMacchia's counsel.  It was excellent
in the address of the broader issues at stake with respect to sysop liability,
and possible improper use of criminal statutes by prosecutors.  

However, I think this is a very poor test case for electronic freedoms.  I
am almost always an avowed enemy of any sort of "conspiracy" or RICO charge
stemming from any crime.  They are typically used on fishing expeditions, or
to nab (mostly) innocent people who can't be proved to have actually done 
anything (like in the Branch Davidian trials).  But in this case, I think if
there ever was such a thing as conspiracy to commit fraud, LaMacchia did 
exactly that, provided the prosecutors' allegations are true.

I would be quick to defend him if he was distributing bestiality .gifs, or
pro-terrorism instructions, or the Skipjack algorithm.  But it seems that he
was facilitating theft, pure and simple.  Any man or woman would be arrested
just as quickly for running a flea market that existed for the express purpose
of fencing stolen property, even if its organizer made no profit from the
illegal activity.  Especially if this organizer advertized "Attention thieves:
flea market customers are looking for Rockford-Fosgate speakers and Ford
Taurus driverside airbags.  Please steal some and bring them to the show."

This is not a liberty issue.  By riding on the coattails of real victims of
government goons, counsel for LaMacchia could bring discredit upon pro-
computer-freedom activists.  There are real sysops (Robert and Coleen Thomas)
facing hundreds of years in prison and millions in fines right this minute
for running a legitimate BBS.  A student spent months in jail with an 
outrageous bail for "anarchy" text files on his legitimate BBS.  IMHO, the 
LaMacchia case is not a basket to put all your eggs in.  It's *NOT* a good
test case for electronic freedom.  Because if prosecutor's allegations are
true, the defendant is just a thief, not a First Amendment defender.  He
might as well have been trafficing in stolen home appliances.  

Using "sysop liability" in this case sullies that defense for folks who
really need it (like sysops who *accidentally* upload pirated software, or
sysops whose BBS is used for illicit purposes without their encouragement).

