The issue for me here is this:

     If I walk into Egghead, and while I'm engaging a sales
 clerk in conversation, someone else takes that opportunity to 
 walk out with a bunch of software, without paying for it, I
 would take it amiss if I were charged with aiding and abetting 
 that theft, because I had (inadvertently) distracted the sales 
 person.

     On the other hand, if I stand outside Egghead, and 
*invite* someone else to help themselves to Egghead's software
while I'm engaging the sales clerk in conversation, and I go in
and have that conversation, and while I'm doing that the other
person does go in and takes that opportunity to walk out with
software without paying for it, then it's hard to see how I'm
not some kind of accessory, and perhaps a participant in (if not
the organizer of) a criminal conspiracy.

     So if you run a computer site, and you provide disk space
for people to use, that in and of itself shouldn't be enough to
put you at risk if your customers engage in illegal activity
using that machine.  That's sort of the principle behind why
sysops shouldn't read other people's mail.

     But if you run a computer site, and you advertise it as a
way to distribute copyrighted software *without* the copyright
owner's permission, then I think you should bear some
responsibility if people take you up on the offer.

