It would also be far more likely. *IF* the facts of the indictment
are true as presented, that the student deliberately set up a site
to encourage trading of pirated software, I suspect that most schools
would expel the student *AND* give their evidence to the police or other
law enforcement agencies.

Personally, if someone used one of OUR systems to do that, I would be
very angry. I'd feel that they had risked our reputation and our net
access, and possibly risked having our machines confiscated, for private
purposes that are at odds with our educational mission.

It is very shabby to use school property to facilitate stealing. 
That's not what we are here to teach. It is a violation of trust.

Again, they've got to prove that the student deliberately did this, and
there are all sorts of important issues surrounding the questions of
bringing our legal system into the cyberspace age. But, if the student did
what he is accused of, I would be damned if I let that student anywhere
NEAR any machine in my system again. If all the charges are true, this was
a full-scale trading post, not just some kid with a copy of something for
personal use.

