In post, Anne Levinson Penway <U35907%UICVM.BITNET@uga.cc.uga.edu> said:

>Below is quoted a segment of a policy written, according to Mr. Noring,
>after the Fanny Hill flap had died down a bit:

>>"** Files NOT Accepted at ftp.cica.indiana.edu
>>
>>As is a standard practice with most anonymous-ftp sites, certain
>>programs, files, or software are outside the mission of archive
>>operations, and therefore will NOT be selected for inclusion in the
>>collection.  These files can include (but are not limited to) any
>>software: containing prejudiced or discriminatory material related to
>>(but not limited to) religion, politics, race, ethnicity, gender,
>>sexuality, etc.; that is adult or sexually-explicit in nature; or
>>otherwise considered unsuitable by the site moderator."


>The whole thing struck me as a classic case of the tail wagging the dog. Did
>CICA have a practice of allowing anyone who got access to upload anything?

My understanding from having uploaded files to CICA before I uploaded
"Fanny Hill", the answer is an unequivocal yes.  Of course, Mr. Regoli would
then go through the uploaded files and determine which ones would be put
into the archive and which would be rejected.  As far as I could tell, his
criterion for accepting or rejecting an item was based solely on whether it
was Windows-specific.  Prior to "Fanny Hill", I had uploaded "Aesop's Fables"
in WinHelp format, and this file was accepted, and is still to be found on
the archive.  In addition, I recently uploaded the "Devil's Dictionary", and
it has been accepted and can be found on the permanent archive (both of these
works are obviously not very controversial).

It is therefore obvious that Fanny Hill was removed from the incoming area
of the archive based on an outside request to do so because of content, before
it was evaluated by Mr. Regoli the same way he evaluated other materials for
inclusion.  And, it is very clear that there was no written policy which
specifically dealt with the content issue (other than if a file is clearly
illegal to be there, e.g., licensed software, children's pornographic images,
etc.), thus there was no written policy basis by which Mr. Regoli could have
rejected "Fanny Hill".  He did so strictly out of fear, in my opinion.


>Were there any generally understood restrictions? If the CICA had established
>a practice of accepting e-text books, for example, and like files, and if
>the CICA is presumed to be a unit of or an agent of the publicly supported
>university, then it seems pretty obvious that the above policy, and the
>original reasons Mr. Regoli gave for removing _Fanny Hill_ don't hold up, and
>the above quoted policy violates the First Amendment, not only by setting
>up impermissible viewpoint discrimination, but vesting the sole authority
>for determining what gets excluded in one individual, with *no guidelines,
>standards or criteria*.

Excellent observation, which I totally agree with.  My previous paragraph
touches upon some of these questions.  And, I believe it is quite a stretch
to assume that CICA is *not* a unit or agent of Indiana University, a publicly
supported university.


>Libraries operate under written materials selection  and/or collection
>development policies that define the library's mission and the scope
>of its collection,  as well as the criteria for determing what will
>be included. Almost all such policies include language about the
>guiding intellectual freedom principles -- that materials will not
>be excluded due to partisan or doctrinal disapproval, and that
>the library will strive to provide a diverse collection representing
>differing and various points of view. Libraries which operate consistent
>with ALA policy don't exclude materials based upon perceived or potential
>offensiveness, and the ALA supports the rights of libraries to collect and
>make available any work they may legally acquire -- that is anything not
>previously declared illegal and outside the bounds of First Amendment
>protection by a court of law...

Based on this paragraph, it seems clear to me that the CICA archive, which
is a library for all intents and purposes, did not adhere to these
guidelines, and I believe their current policy (reproduced above) does not
either.


>On the other hand, there are a lot of objective
>reasons why things don't get into library collections -- a combination of
>lack of reviews, no information about author or publisher, budget, space,
>service population, need, redundancy, etc. *But none of these may permissibly
>be used as a smokescreen for censorship. If the motivation is to suppress
>constitutionally protected expression deemed "offensive," then that's
>censorship.*

Since my other electronic books have been accepted and are part of the CICA
archive (which I call a library), then the only reason that "Fanny Hill" is
not there is because of a complaint from an individual in New Zealand about
the "content" of it, the content of which was declared "not obscene" by the
U.S. Supreme Court in 1966.  (By the way, it is the threat from someone
halfway around the world, in a different country where social censorship
is more heavily practiced, that makes this case even more maddening to me.)


>The _Fanny Hill_ saga illustrated that system administrators/operators
>might benefit from the experience of librarians in selection of materials
>and collection management. In Mr. Regoli's defense, it doesn't appear that
>he had any opportunity to make an objective analysis of whether this
>submission to the archive was within its mission and scope, and then
>he blew it by saying exactly the *wrong* thing about why it was pulled
>off. If the archive has been letting anything at all go up, and then
>pulling it off after someone complains or because they are worried about
>offensiveness, they are not only doing things backwards but contrary to
>long established First Amendment and intellectual freedom principles.

Mr. Regoli has done an outstanding job at creating and building the CICA
archive to one of the best of its kind in the world.  Indiana University
should be proud of it.  And it is fast becoming the standard by which others
will emulate.  Thus, in my opinion, it is critical that Indiana University
be made aware of the implications and ramifications of their elite electronic
library not having a robust and progressive, non-censorship "selection"
policy.  The one that Mr. Regoli has instituted (reproduced above) is there
because of his "fear" of what Indiana University could do, which, in his
opinion, is close down the CICA archive.

That's why IU, not Mr. Regoli, needs to write the policy, and I really urge
the ALA to contact the administration at IU about this and offer their help.
I don't believe that my contacting IU will get anywhere -- it takes a respected
organization, such as ALA, to do this.  And the benefit to ALA is that once a
good policy is written for IU's electronic library, this same policy can then
become a template by which other electronic libraries can implement, knowing
there is an actual organization out there devoted to intellectual freedom.

And note that ALA *will* have to deal with this issue sooner or later.  I
believe the CICA situation is a golden opportunity -- missing out on this will
just make it a little harder down the road when the number of electronic
libraries, running under faulty policies (some of which may actually
duplicate CICA's current one!), will greatly increase.  Nip it in the bud now
while it is still easy to do so.


>The above quoted policy starts out okay, trying to define the mission
>of the archive, but stumbles  very quickly into impermissible, discriminatory
>criteria -- who gets to decide what's "prejudicial or discriminatory?"

My thoughts exactly.  I'm sure that IU had nothing to do with policy -- any
attorney or conscientious administrator representing IU would have heart
palpitations over this one.


>Motivation is crucial in First Amendment analysis - if the material was
>removed because a government official or agent *disagreed with the ideas
>expressed, and/or found the ideas personally offensive and used the official
>position to suppress or exclude them on that basis, then that official has
>acted outside the bounds of his authority and violated the First Amendment.

This apparently did not happen here (though I'm not sure -- maybe the person
in NZ did carry out his threat and contact IU staff about it, and then the
staff asked Mr. Regoli to "settle it" -- this is only speculation on my part).
But it was obviously due to the fear Mr. Regoli had that IU would shut down
his archive (he said this -- doesn't this show that IU has defacto control of
the archive?) should they perceive the archive as more trouble than it is
worth.  I disagree with Mr. Regoli's assessment here, but nevertheless that
is what Mr. Regoli believes, and his current policy is the written expression
of his inward fears.


>If just about anything was going up on the archive, and there was
>no policy at all defining what the archive is for and what it is not
>for in terms that did not set up impermissible viewpoint discrimination,
>(e.g., you can't say, we'll allow stuff about religion, but only "non-
>preudiced" stuff,) and if the archive is supported by public funds and
>is a unit or agent of the public university, it seems arguable that
>Mr. Regoli has created a kind of limited public forum, and may not
>exclude materials based on the viewpoint expressed.

My thoughts exactly.  See what I've written above.


>If I've missed something or have been misinformed, or if anyone thinks I have
>misinterpreted the situation, please jump in here. I'm always
>open to discussion and criticism. I'd be interested in hearing from
>university librarians and system administrators on this, because this
>is an area that's still pretty much up for grabs, policy-wise.

I believe you are the first at ALA to grasp the situation correctly.


In conclusion, let me say that ALA should write a "generic" policy statement
for use by electronic libraries/archives based in the U.S., such as CICA's.
This generic policy statement then would be promoted as a template to all
new electronic archives that will get started throughout the country
(remember, it is obvious that over the next few years the number of publicly
run electronic libraries will exponentially grow.)  In addition, I again
urge ALA to formally contact the IU administration and offer ALA's services
to help write a sound policy for the CICA archive.

And Anne, thanks for your informed reply.  I hope that ALA will consider my
requests.

Jon Noring


