I'm pleased to report a positive development in this matter.  The
Swedish government today (April 14th) decided that Anders R Olsson
doesn't need any license or permit from the Data Inquisition in
order to write a book on his personal computer, even though it may
include personal information.  It's not clear to me whether this
decision also makes him exempt from having a license for gathering
research material in machine-readable form, in preparation for his
books.  The future may tell.  In any case, this seems to show that
the Freedom of Print Act of 1949 takes precedence over the Data Act
of 1973 when it comes to computerized book manuscripts, something
which unfortunately hasn't been entirely obvious until now.

Note that this isn't a court decision on the constitutional legality
of the Data Act, but a decision by the government on the rulings of
a public authority (the DI) in a particular case.

I hope this decision also paves the way for non-printed statements
of opinion containing personal information (such as electronic mail)
to eventually be declared exempt from the regulations of the Swedish
Data Act, for the sake of freedom of expression.  We'll have to find
a more reasonable way to protect the privacy of individuals than by
outlawing machine-readable information.  Any ideas?
