Copyright (c) 1994 by Communications Voir Inc.  All Rights Reserved.
Reprinted with permission.


Montreal Hour
Thursday, April 14, 1994
vol.2 no.11 page 7


		Restricted Rights
		-----------------

   Proposed changes to McGill's student code allow
   warrantless searches and students accused of
   plagiarism could be "guilty unless proven innocent".

      "We may as well say that democracy ends at the
      [McGill's] Roddick gates -- Lawyer Julius Grey


by Matt Friedman and Marlene Blanshay


Students at McGill University have more than their impending
finals to worry about as the school year draws to a close.
McGill's Senate met last week to discuss two separate initiatives,
one which would create new restrictions for users of the
university's computer system, and another that would greatly
increase disciplinary powers under McGill's student code of conduct.

One of the most controversial changes is the revision to Article 40
of the code, which states that, at a disciplinary hearing, relevant
but illegally obtained evidence would be admissible.  A memo,
apparently circulated by the university's legal counsel in February,
claimed that university officials had the right to search anything,
anywhere on campus.

The proposed revision to the student code would also grant the
university extensive new powers in investigations and disciplinary
hearings against students, yet vice-principal Francois Tavenas
characterizes the changes as nothing more than a routine update.

"The university environment is changing", he says.  "Our policies
have to reflect that.  We had identified a number of elements in
the existing code that needed revision, and the Dean of Students
thought the time was right for a review."

However, there is concern on campus that students' rights to
privacy will be violated should the changes be ratified.
Constitutional lawyer Julius Grey, a McGill professor, says
he is "dumbfounded" by the proposed changes.  "This is an
attempt to toughen the regulations and reduce procedural
safeguards -- to make it easier to accuse", he says.  "I don't
understand the reasoning behind the revisions.  What is
so wrong at McGill -- has there been an epidemic of violence,
or a rash of cheating?"

"It's Kafkaesque and preposterous," adds Grey.  "If [the right
to search] opinions are correct, then we may as well say that
democracy ends at the [McGill's] Roddick Gates.  Keeping in mind
that much of the disciplinary action might go on in [student]
residences, this seems to be an invitation to tape conversations,
snoop, and search people's private rooms.

   --  The law is the law  --

Jill Presser, a law student and the associate director of McGill's
Legal Information Clinic, has also expressed concern about the code
changes and their possible effect on students' rights to privacy.
"The law is the law", she says.  "You can't violate the law because
McGill is a discrete community with its own rules and Article 1 of
the Charter of Student Rights guarantees all students the rights
and freedoms recognized by law."

The revisions include provisions for the university to expel a student
for up to 30 days before bringing him or her to a disciplinary hearing
(increased from the current 15-day maximum), and an extension of the
"presumption of guilty" in cases of plagiarism.  Under the revised code,
not only will a student accused of plagiarism be presumed guilty, but
so will anyone accused of selling schoolwork to another student who
submits it as his or her own.

Grey appreciates that plagiarism could threaten the university's
academic integrity, but points out that McGill has previously dealt
with plagiarism without expanding the presumption of guilt, and he
warns that even the existing provisions should be carefully restricted.

   -- Capital crimes --

But Ron Sklar, the law professor who drafted the original student
code in 1981, says "plagiarism is a capital crime at a university.
The presumption of guilt was the most hotly-debated part of the
original code.  The reasoning is that, in cases of plagiarism,
we can presume an intent to deceive."

Sklar is confident that the revisions are intended to make the
disciplinary process more efficient, and not to impose some kind
of draconian rule.  But, he adds, "as a matter of policy, the
university should not extend its powers to the very limits of
what is legal.  You can say you have a power for exceptional
cases, but it's not wise to exercise it."

The appearance of a crackdown is underscored by the draft code
of conduct for computer users, the first in McGill's history.
While its development has been entirely incidental to the student
code, and was motivated by what Tavenas calls a lag between the
technology and the rules that govern it, it nonetheless raises
many of the same issues.

At the heart of the computer code controversy is, again, the
question of privacy rights.  Presser, who sits on the Senate
sub-committee delegated to draft the code, points out that while
the privacy of electronic mail and other activities is assured
in the code, it is contradicted by another proposed changes to
the student code which baldly state that a student "may not claim
that communications made through the university computer system
are confidential ..."

   -- Protecting privacy --

Although Presser agrees authorities may have to violate user
privacy to protect the security of the computer system, she says
"privacy should be the default, and anything else considered
exceptional -- and any exceptional circumstances should be justified."

Presser tried to convince the subcommittee that, in all but the
most extreme cases, any intrusive investigation on the computer
should first be approved by a specially constituted body that
"would hear the allegations and come to a majority decision on
whether to have an investigation.  This would be to ensure the
decision to investigate is not made arbitrarily for capriciously.
However, the subcommittee disagreed and decided that should only
be necessary when a system manager made a `substantive jump',
and not when he was `just following a trail'."

Presser believes that this distinction is too subtle, and
could allow computer system managers to arbitrarily violate
users' privacy.  "There is a danger that someone may say he
