To All University Faculty Members and Fellow Students,

I am a student in the Department of Mechanical Engineering at the Technion -
Israel Institute of Technology.  I am also a member of the Technion
Academic Committee. This Committee consists of four students and four
professors, and is responsible for planning agendas and proposals for the
Technion's governing body, the "Senate". The Committee is attended by the
Dean of Students, the Dean of Undergraduate Studies, the Technion
President's Assistant for Academic Affairs, and various other Technion
VIP's (who are not Committee members). The President of the Technion drops
in from time to time. 

As part of my duties on the committee, I have set out to check the
three following subjects:

1.      University and pan-university competitions as a base for
getting technical hands-on experience and general stimulation of
interest in their studies. For example, the Bi-Annual Solar Vehicle
Competition in Australia. (In this competition, teams of students
build vehicles powered by solar energy and race them.) I am
interested in any information regarding this type of
technologically-based competition. 

2.      Computer simulators to assist learning and
demonstration of basic technical terms. I have heard of a computer
simulation of power plants that was devised to accompany a basic
course in thermodynamics.  At this time it is possible to pass a
basic course in thermodynamics with out knowing what a turbine
blade looks like.  Any type of computer simulator of relevance to
technical studies, such as those taught at the Technion, is of
interest to me. 

3.      Lunch breaks. Don't laugh! I have heard that a forced lunch
break for all students (1 hour daily) does wonders to student
morale. The idea we are looking into is to organize food stands,
street shows etc. One of the problems students face in the Technion
is a bad feeling towards the campus in general and campus life in
particular. The students develop such negative feelings about the
Technion that the alumni organization is almost non existent (how
different from the States!). Any academic-oriented ideas for
boosting student's feeling of togetherness are welcomed.


I would be grateful for any relevant information, ideas, enlightning
remarks, and so on to help me advance these proposals.  I would
also appreciate hearing from students on comparable committees in
their own universities.

